nstitute for Health and Care Excellence (Nice) on Wednesday night.<br><br>
Charities celebrated a =E2=80=9Ca momentous day for thousands of people=E2=
=80=9D who would have =E2=80=9Cprecious extra time with loved ones=E2=80=9D=
. The treatment, which extends life by six months on average, will be avail=
able in England from Thursday.<br><br><strong>Why wasn=E2=80=99t the drug a=
vailable sooner?</strong> Enhertu was rejected for use on the NHS in Englan=
d in 2024 because it was too expensive. Under Nice=E2=80=99s criteria at th=
e time, metastatic breast cancer was not =E2=80=9Csevere=E2=80=9D enough to=
 justify the cost.&nbsp;<br><br>
This led to a global row because the drug was available in a host of Europe=
an countries, including Scotland. Sir Pascal Soriot, the chief executive of=
 AstraZeneca, called on the NHS to change its drug funding system.<br><br>I=
n April, the price criteria by which drugs are deemed cost-effective for th=
e NHS was altered in response to Trump=E2=80=99s threat to impose a 100 per=
 cent tariff on pharmaceutical exports entering the US unless the UK spent =
more on medicine.<br><br>
<strong>How does it work?</strong> Enhertu, which is given via a drip in ho=
spital every three weeks, is suitable for women with HER2-low breast cancer=
 that has spread to other parts of the body. It is not a cure but can exten=
d life.<br><br>It is part of a class of drugs, known as antibody-drug conju=
gates, that hunt down breast cancer cells and bind to a protein on their su=
rface called HER-2 before delivering a powerful dose of chemotherapy direct=
ly to the tumour.&nbsp;</span></span></p></div>
