NoZWQtMHNycndscW5y/6a464fcf7d55e1fcfb02eb9cBc5708696" target=3D"_blank" sty=
le=3D"text-decoration: underline; color: #006699;" rel=3D"noopener">deep cu=
ltural problem</a> that deserves our attention. We have become a society th=
at instinctively blames rather than understands. The result is a climate in=
 which admitting error is treated as a career-ending act rather than a resp=
onsible one. The Letby case demonstrates this. Senior clinicians who raised=
 legitimate concerns were pressured to apologise, while management prioriti=
sed institutional image over patient safety. This was the predictable behav=
iour of leaders operating in a culture where admitting fault invites vilifi=
cation. If we want safer hospitals and safer governance, we must cultivate =
a more tolerant civic environment =E2=80=94 one that distinguishes between =
error and wrongdoing, and encourages early admission of problems rather tha=
n punishing it.</span>
